940 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (As profit maximising firms, oligopolists collude to use their market power to set prices high. The incentive to collude occurs because firms will lose revenue if they raise price \(elastic demand\) or lower price \(inelastic demand\), meaning they do not compete on price. This can be further explained using the Prisoners Dilemma, where firms can maximise joint welfare by colluding at High, High. This means consumers suffer from reduced consumer surplus. Prices may rise even further over time as the lack of competitive pressure can lead to firms being X-inefficient, producing above their lowest attainable average cost. Firms deliberately restrict output to keep prices high - this means consumers suffer from allocative inefficiency, with too few resources allocated to the bicycle market. Consumers suffer from poor quality bicycles as the lack of competition means firms have no incentive to be dynamically efficient and invest in)
/E (As profit maximising firms, oligopolists collude to use their market power to set prices high. The incentive to collude occurs because firms will lose revenue if they raise price \(elastic demand\) or lower price \(inelastic demand\), meaning they do not compete on price. This can be further explained using the Prisoners Dilemma, where firms can maximise joint welfare by colluding at High, High. This means consumers suffer from reduced consumer surplus. Prices may rise even further over time as the lack of competitive pressure can lead to firms being X-inefficient, producing above their lowest attainable average cost. Firms deliberately restrict output to keep prices high - this means consumers suffer from allocative inefficiency, with too few resources allocated to the bicycle market. Consumers suffer from poor quality bicycles as the lack of competition means firms have no incentive to be dynamically efficient and invest in)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
